This C program continuously reads the analog output of a Seeed Studio Grove Rain Sensor through an MCP3008 on SPI channel 0 configured at 1 MHz mode 0, polls the sensor once per second, appends the rain level together with a timestamp to a file named `rain_log.txt`, and based on a default rain-threshold value of 5 decides whether to close or open a window. When the measured level exceeds the threshold, the program drives a motor forward for exactly three seconds using the L298N-based DC motor driver with IN1 connected to GPIO17 (BCM), IN2 to GPIO18 (BCM), and ENA (PWM) to GPIO27 (BCM) at a PWM duty of 150 within a range of 255. Otherwise, it drives the motor in reverse for three seconds. The window is assumed initially open. The code checks every initialization, system call, and I/O return value, handling failures to initialize the driver library, open the SPI device or the log file by printing clear error messages and performing proper cleanup. It also detects and logs any SPI transfer or file-write errors without crashing. Signal handlers for SIGINT and SIGTERM set a volatile `sig_atomic_t` flag that causes the main loop to stop the motor, close the SPI interface, close the log file, and terminate the driver library before exiting. All resources are released on any exit path. The program should be compiled with `gcc -o rain rain.c -lthe driver library -lrt -lpthread`.

