This C program interfaces with a TI AWR1642 mmWave radar sensor over SPI channel 0 (CE0/GPIO8) on a Raspberry Pi 4 Model B. It configures the SPI at 1 MHz, mode 0, and uses a hardware frame trigger on GPIO23 (BCM) and a data-ready interrupt on GPIO24 (BCM) to capture range-Doppler heatmap frames at a 100 ms period. The sensor is pre-configured via the TI mmWave SDK to stream range-Doppler data, with a configuration struct that records a start frequency of 77 GHz, end frequency of 79 GHz, 64 chirps, and 256 samples. Each frame requires a full-duplex SPI transfer of 32,768 bytes of heatmap data, which is logged to a binary file. The header of the binary file contains the documented magic number 4144, an 8-byte timestamp, and the configuration struct, followed by per-frame timestamps and frame numbers. The program checks all return codes, handles any memory allocation failures, and ensures that on receipt of SIGINT or SIGTERM, it gracefully closes the SPI device, releases the GPIO pins, and closes the log file.

