This C program continuously monitors the coolant flow rate from a Sensirion SLF3S-1300F Liquid Flow Sensor attached to I2C bus 1 on a Raspberry Pi Zero W. It issues the sensor command, waits 2 ms before the first read, and then samples once per second. Each read retrieves exactly three bytes – a 2-byte big-endian signed 16-bit flow value followed by a 1-byte CRC-8 calculated with the documented polynomial and initial value. The program validates the CRC, flags a read as an error if the CRC fails, and also verifies that the converted flow rate lies within the plausible range of 0.0 to 100.0 ml/min, marking out-of-range values with a status of INVALID. For every successful measurement, it logs a line to a comma-separated file named `cooling_log.csv` containing the timestamp, the flow rate in ml/min, the raw sensor flags, and a computed status string. It writes the header "Timestamp,Flow Rate (ml/min),Status" only when the file is created for the first time. For any I2C read failure or CRC error, it logs a row with status ERROR and attempts recovery by re-initializing the I2C handle. All driver/I2C calls and file operations are checked for errors, and the program handles SIGINT (Ctrl+C) to perform a graceful shutdown that closes the I2C handle, closes the CSV file, and terminates any driver resources on every exit path.

